---
title: "stdlib-bloom"
author: bad-antics
date: 2026-04-05
abstract: Classical bit-array Bloom filter with double-hashing on top of two FNV-1a variants. Covers construction, insertion, membership testing, optimal parameter selection, and false-positive rate.

# Construction

new_filter(m, k) creates an empty filter with m bits and k hash functions. optimal_params(n, fp_rate) picks m and k for an expected element count n and target false-positive rate.

// Create a filter with 1024 bits and 4 hashes
let f = new_filter(1024, 4)

// Pick optimal params for 1000 elements at 1% FP rate
let (m, k) = optimal_params(1000, 0.01)

# Insertion

insert adds an element to the filter by setting k bits derived from double-hashing.

// Insert an element
f = insert(f, "hello")
f = insert(f, "world")

# Membership Test

contains reports whether an element might be in the set. A true result may be a false positive; a false result is definitive.

// Test membership
contains(f, "hello")   // true
contains(f, "absent")  // false (definitive)

# Canonical Vectors

Fixed test vectors (m=16, k=3):

- new_filter(16, 3) -> count 0, contains any element false
- insert "a" -> contains "a" true
- insert "a", "b" -> contains "a" true, contains "b" true, contains "c" false
- optimal_params(100, 0.01) -> m around 959, k around 7

# Executable Program

fn main() {
    let f = new_filter(1024, 4)
    f = insert(f, "hello")
    f = insert(f, "world")
    print(contains(f, "hello"))
    print(contains(f, "absent"))
}

  </script>
  <script src="../ltlml.js"></script>
